ta okay let's assume Bernie  Sanders has a net worth of a million  dollars just to pick a number maybe it  ends up being more I don't know Bernie  Sanders is 77 years old okay 1 million  dollar net worth at age 77 is not  statistically unusual this is not my  opinion  I'm just telling you what the data is if  you look at 2016 Federal Reserve data  for people in the United States age 75  to 79 the mean wealth is 233 thousand  dollars and the median wealth is 1.1  million I am not making a comment on  anything other then for Bernie's age  it's a fact that half of the country has  more wealth than Bernie and half of the  country has less wealth than Bernie  that's what it means that Bernie  basically has the median net worth for  someone his age so when we talk about  Senators with 40 million dollars in all  of this Bernie Sanders is not in that  league okay he's been working for 60  years he's been earning money a good  salary as a member of the House of  Representatives and more recently the  Senate presumably he's been saving money  compound interest is a thing and I know  that the term millionaire is very loaded  and in a sense it shouldn't be in this  particular case if Bernie's net worth is  1 million dollars or 1.1 million dollars  he's in the fiftieth percentile for  people his age using the median as our  indicator those numbers are also 3 years  old meaning that 1.1 million today might  more closely be the 48th percentile or  the 46th percentile I've not seen 2019  data so that's number one all right but  I don't even care about that we should  know the numbers but there's a really  more important issue here you can be  poor and advocate for policies that help  the rich look at the 2010 Tea Party  movement you had people who weren't rich  and we're likely never going to be rich  pushing for tax cuts for the rich and  cuts to capital gains taxes and stuff  that would never affect them in the same  way you can be median wealthy or rich or  whatever and advocate